<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheyenne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tsitsistas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cheyenne are an Algonquian-speaking Indigenous people of the Great Plains, who moved west from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> region and took up a horse-based bison-hunting life in the 18th century. Allied with the Lakota, they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the wars of the 1860s and 1870s, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a> in 1876. Today they are divided into the Northern Cheyenne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and the Southern Cheyenne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheyenne.jpg" alt="Cheyenne" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilcrease_-_Cheyenne_Beaded_Dress.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 18th century the Lakota obtained horses from the <b>Cheyenne</b> along the Missouri River, calling them šuŋkawakaŋ, “dog of power”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lakota, Northern <b>Cheyenne</b> and Arapaho warriors killed Lieutenant Colonel George Custer and some 268 soldiers in Montana Territory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-25">25 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The 7th Cavalry under George Armstrong Custer attacked a <b>Cheyenne</b> village on the Washita River, killing Black Kettle and many women and children.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-27">27 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After gold was found in the Black Hills in 1874, the Great Sioux War followed; Lakota and <b>Cheyenne</b> under Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
